<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jin (Five Dynasties)</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 923 CE · capital Taiyuan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Shatuo Turkic principality of Li Keyong and Li Cunxu at Taiyuan, the Later Liang's great rival, which conquered it and became the Later Tang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a>, helped the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 883 and was rewarded with the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedong-shanxi">Hedong</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. He was made Prince of Jin in 895. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> ended the Tang and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> in 907, Li Keyong refused to submit.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jin was a military state built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> and other cavalry. It continued to use the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> reign title as a sign of loyalty to the old dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, succeeding in 908, defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> at Bochao in 911, annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yan-five-dynasties">Yan (Five Dynasties)</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> in 913 and won most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> proclaimed himself emperor of a restored <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> at Daming and within months captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a>. Jin thus became the Later Tang, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a> went on to found three of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jin-five-dynasties.jpg" alt="Jin (Five Dynasties)" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Jin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 923 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taiyuan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Keyong, Li Cunxu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Shatuo Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jin (Hedong), Jin (907-923)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=jin-five-dynasties" aria-label="Jin (Five Dynasties) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7487 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jin (Five Dynasties) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7807 1072L7898 1125M7807 1072L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Taiyuan. <a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=jin-five-dynasties">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
